# Worktree notebooks

Each parallel worktree gets its own development notebook. Run Jupyter from that worktree so the notebook exercises that worktree's code and environment.

- Create or use a worktree-local exploratory notebook (Jupytext pairs `ipynb` with `py:percent`).
- Start Jupyter from the worktree root (after `uv sync`) so the kernel uses that worktree's `.venv` and sources.
- Do not modify shared story/demo notebooks (especially `demo_story.ipynb`) from a parallel worktree.
- After the worktree is merged, use the exploratory notebooks and commits to update `demo_story.ipynb`.

```text
# BAD — editing the shared demo from a feature worktree
worktrees/my-feature/demo_story.ipynb

# GOOD — local exploration in the worktree, demo updated after merge
worktrees/my-feature/scratch_<topic>.ipynb
# merge, then update demo_story.ipynb on the main worktree
```
